From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: desp <me@desp.lol>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 04:36:42 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 19/39] Shaders: one back edge per block, for vkd3d-proton

The console flow's nested loops leave an inner loop's latch straight to
the outer loop's header: one block that branches back to two headers.
dxil-spirv keeps one back edge per block and takes the other for a
forward edge. In a program with WaveReadLaneAt its shuffle pass
(flatten_subgroup_shuffles) analyses the CFG before anything rewrites
extra back edges, and its loop-exit search (LoopMergeTracer) recurses
over that cycle without end: the pipeline thread overflows its stack, or
with a large stack eats memory until the process dies (fdd56d97a806bcca
on #439, which adds the lane reads; seen natively, 40 GB in 40 s).

single_latches (#1885, one back edge per header) now also sends such a
block's edge to the outer header through a new block of its own, so the
block stays the inner loop's exiting latch, as in DXC's loops. The
write-time fold of empty latches no longer folds one into a block that
already branches back, which would undo that.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 91e3bdfd8f57b2917311583a99d676cc56b0c807)
(cherry picked from commit e2157e6c066c353421fe99a5357f30c60651808d)

